This one is not a Reactor retry, so I would handle it differently.

Here the retry is a manual while loop, so a RetryConfig or Reactor doBeforeRetry() won't help. You already have a retry counter, so the simplest approach is to add proper exception logging inside the catch and log the final failure when retries are exhausted.

Recommended change

Current:

} catch (PersistenceException ex) {
    if (ex.getCause() instanceof ConstraintViolationException
            || ex.getMessage().contains(NotificationResourceCommonConstant.SUBSCRIPTION_EVENT_CONSTRAINT_NAME)) {

        // ...
        retryAttempts++;

        log.warn(new LogBuilder().withMessage("Attempt: " + retryAttempts
                + " to save Subscription Events failed due to constraint violation. Retrying...").toString());
    } else {
        throw ex;
    }
}

Change it to log the actual exception details:

} catch (PersistenceException ex) {
    if (ex.getCause() instanceof ConstraintViolationException
            || (ex.getMessage() != null
            && ex.getMessage().contains(
                NotificationResourceCommonConstant.SUBSCRIPTION_EVENT_CONSTRAINT_NAME))) {

        retryAttempts++;

        log.warn(
                new LogBuilder()
                        .withMessage("Retrying save of Subscription Events after failure")
                        .withField("retryAttempt", retryAttempts)
                        .withField("maxRetries", MAX_RETRIES)
                        .withField("exceptionClass", ex.getClass().getName())
                        .withField("message", ex.getMessage())
                        .withStackTrace(ex.getStackTrace())
                        .toString(),
                ex
        );

        // Re-fetch latest events and filter out already existing ones
        subscriptionEvents.clear();
        newSubscriptionEvents.clear();

        newSubscriptionEvents = fetchExistingAndCreateNewSubscriptionEvents(
                notificationInfoRequestDto.getNotifyingStatusList(),
                subscriptionEvents
        );

    } else {
        log.error(
                new LogBuilder()
                        .withMessage("Failed to save Subscription Events")
                        .withField("exceptionClass", ex.getClass().getName())
                        .withField("message", ex.getMessage())
                        .withStackTrace(ex.getStackTrace())
                        .toString(),
                ex
        );
        throw ex;
    }
}

Then improve the exhausted case:

if (retryAttempts == MAX_RETRIES) {
    log.error(
            new LogBuilder()
                    .withMessage("Retries exhausted while saving Subscription Events")
                    .withField("totalRetries", retryAttempts)
                    .toString()
    );

    throw new NotificationGenericException(
            "Failed to save Subscription Events after "
                    + MAX_RETRIES
                    + " attempts."
    );
}

The resulting flow

DB save
  ↓
PersistenceException
  ↓
Is it a retryable constraint violation?
  ↓ YES
retryAttempts++
  ↓
LOG exception + attempt number
  ↓
retry
  ↓
Exception again
  ↓
LOG attempt 2
  ↓
...
  ↓
MAX_RETRIES reached
  ↓
LOG retries exhausted
  ↓
throw NotificationGenericException

One important point

This retry is not the same category as your Reactor-based WebClient retries:

WebClient retry
    → Reactor RetryConfig
    → doBeforeRetry()

DB retry
    → manual while loop
    → log inside catch

So I would not create a common retry configuration just for this method. The retry logic is tightly coupled to the database constraint handling, and the existing retryAttempts already provides the information needed for logging.

Also, notice the small defensive fix:

ex.getMessage() != null

before calling .contains(...); otherwise a null exception message could cause another NullPointerException while handling the original failure.

For this card, I would describe this as adding retry-attempt and final-failure logging to the existing manual retry mechanism, rather than converting it to a listener.